# Codebase organization

Build so work moves fast **now** and a professional team can take over **later** without a rewrite.

## Structure

- Follow the established layout — do not invent parallel patterns:
  - `frontend/src/` — UI, pages, components, hooks
  - `backend/src/api/` — routes only; thin handlers
  - `backend/src/services/` — business logic
  - `backend/src/scrapers/{supplier}/` — one folder per supplier
  - `backend/src/workers/` — background jobs
  - `backend/supabase/migrations/` — schema changes only here
- New features go in the **correct layer** on day one — no "temporary" files in random places.
- Add a short `README.md` when creating a new top-level module (what it does, main entry file).

## Iteration-friendly, handover-ready

- **Small, focused changes** — one feature or fix per task; easy to review.
- **Obvious entry points** — `main`, route files, worker registrars, scraper registry. No hunting.
- **Config in env** — URLs, keys, feature flags via `.env.example`; never hardcode secrets or supplier URLs in logic.
- **Types at boundaries** — API request/response, DB rows, scraper output, LLM tool params.
- **Consistent patterns** — if one scraper uses `parser.ts` + `adapter.ts`, the next one does too.
- **No prototype debt** — skip shortcuts that block scale (god files, inline SQL in routes, copy-paste adapters).

## Documentation sync

- If structure or flows change, update the matching file in `.cursor/docs/`.
- New env vars → document in `.env.example` with a one-line comment.

## Before merging work

- [ ] New code is in the right folder, not bolted on
- [ ] A new developer could run the app from README + `.env.example`
- [ ] No orphaned files, duplicate utilities, or unexplained `utils/misc.ts`
- [ ] Architecture docs still match reality (or were updated)
